 {"edgeAttributes":[{"po":0,"n":"citation","v":["pubmed:20086235"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":0,"n":"mechanism","v":["transcriptional regulation"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":0,"n":"residue","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":0,"n":"sequence","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":0,"n":"cell_data","v":["BTO:0000801"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":0,"n":"tissue_data","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":0,"n":"notes","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":0,"n":"annotator","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":0,"n":"sentence","v":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/20086235\">pubmed:20086235</a> Both NF-κBs bind to a conserved DNA motif (80) that is found in numerous IL-1–responsive genes, in particular the ones encoding IκBα (81), IL-6 (82), IL-8 (18, 83,84), monocyte chemoattractant protein 1 (MCP1) (28), and cyclooxygenase 2 (COX2)"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":0,"n":"direct","v":"false","d":"boolean"}, {"po":1,"n":"citation","v":["pubmed:30060484"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":1,"n":"mechanism","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":1,"n":"residue","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":1,"n":"sequence","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":1,"n":"cell_data","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":1,"n":"tissue_data","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":1,"n":"notes","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":1,"n":"annotator","v":["miannu"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":1,"n":"sentence","v":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/30060484\">pubmed:30060484</a> The bacterial endotoxin LPS is a known agonist of TLR2 that activates the expression of proinflammatory cytokines and the phosphorylation of MAPKs and NFκB [49]. In addition, the activation of the MAPK and NFκB signaling cascades drive inflammation and macrophage polarization. "],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":1,"n":"direct","v":"false","d":"boolean"}, {"po":2,"n":"citation","v":["pubmed:12692549"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":2,"n":"mechanism","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":2,"n":"residue","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":2,"n":"sequence","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":2,"n":"cell_data","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":2,"n":"tissue_data","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":2,"n":"notes","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":2,"n":"annotator","v":["lperfetto"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":2,"n":"sentence","v":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/12692549\">pubmed:12692549</a> The transcription factors interferon regulatory factor 3 (IRF3) and NF-B are required for the expression of many genes involved in the innate immune response."],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":2,"n":"direct","v":"false","d":"boolean"}, {"po":3,"n":"citation","v":["pubmed:20457564"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":3,"n":"mechanism","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":3,"n":"residue","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":3,"n":"sequence","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":3,"n":"cell_data","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":3,"n":"tissue_data","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":3,"n":"notes","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":3,"n":"annotator","v":["gcesareni"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":3,"n":"sentence","v":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/20457564\">pubmed:20457564</a> The nuclear factor NF-kappaB pathway has long been considered a prototypical proinflammatory signaling pathway, largely based on the role of NF-kappaB in the expression of proinflammatory genes including cytokines, chemokines, and adhesion molecules."],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":3,"n":"direct","v":"false","d":"boolean"}, {"po":4,"n":"citation","v":["pubmed:22926518"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":4,"n":"mechanism","v":["binding"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":4,"n":"residue","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":4,"n":"sequence","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":4,"n":"cell_data","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":4,"n":"tissue_data","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":4,"n":"notes","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":4,"n":"annotator","v":["miannu"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":4,"n":"sentence","v":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/22926518\">pubmed:22926518</a> The activated TGFβ type I receptor kinase propagates the signal within the cell through phosphorylation of the receptor-regulated (R-)Smad proteins Smad2 and Smad3 at their extreme carboxyl-terminal serine residues.1, 2 The activated R-Smads then form heteromeric complexes with the common-partner (Co-)Smad, Smad4, and accumulate in the nucleus, where they can bind DNA and regulate gene expression. The Smads control gene expression in a cell type-specific manner by interacting with other proteins, such as AP-1, AP-2 and Ets transcription factors and specific co-activators and co-repressors."],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":4,"n":"direct","v":"true","d":"boolean"}, {"po":5,"n":"citation","v":["pubmed:22926518"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":5,"n":"mechanism","v":["binding"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":5,"n":"residue","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":5,"n":"sequence","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":5,"n":"cell_data","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":5,"n":"tissue_data","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":5,"n":"notes","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":5,"n":"annotator","v":["miannu"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":5,"n":"sentence","v":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/22926518\">pubmed:22926518</a> The activated TGFβ type I receptor kinase propagates the signal within the cell through phosphorylation of the receptor-regulated (R-)Smad proteins Smad2 and Smad3 at their extreme carboxyl-terminal serine residues.1, 2 The activated R-Smads then form heteromeric complexes with the common-partner (Co-)Smad, Smad4, and accumulate in the nucleus, where they can bind DNA and regulate gene expression. The Smads control gene expression in a cell type-specific manner by interacting with other proteins, such as AP-1, AP-2 and Ets transcription factors and specific co-activators and co-repressors."],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":5,"n":"direct","v":"true","d":"boolean"}, {"po":6,"n":"citation","v":["pubmed:32454942"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":6,"n":"mechanism","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":6,"n":"residue","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":6,"n":"sequence","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":6,"n":"cell_data","v":["BTO:0002417"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":6,"n":"tissue_data","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":6,"n":"notes","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":6,"n":"annotator","v":["miannu"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":6,"n":"sentence","v":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/32454942\">pubmed:32454942</a>  interferon gamma- (IFNγ-) and interleukin-17- (IL-17-) secreting CD4+ T cells are believed to be the pathogenic initiators of MS [22], and in MS patients, the increased production of either IFNγ or IL-17 is associated with pathology "],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":6,"n":"direct","v":"false","d":"boolean"}, {"po":7,"n":"citation","v":["pubmed:32454942"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":7,"n":"mechanism","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":7,"n":"residue","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":7,"n":"sequence","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":7,"n":"cell_data","v":["BTO:0002417"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":7,"n":"tissue_data","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":7,"n":"notes","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":7,"n":"annotator","v":["miannu"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":7,"n":"sentence","v":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/32454942\">pubmed:32454942</a>  interferon gamma- (IFNγ-) and interleukin-17- (IL-17-) secreting CD4+ T cells are believed to be the pathogenic initiators of MS [22], and in MS patients, the increased production of either IFNγ or IL-17 is associated with pathology "],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":7,"n":"direct","v":"false","d":"boolean"}, {"po":8,"n":"citation","v":["pubmed:20086235"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":8,"n":"mechanism","v":["transcriptional regulation"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":8,"n":"residue","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":8,"n":"sequence","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":8,"n":"cell_data","v":["BTO:0000801"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":8,"n":"tissue_data","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":8,"n":"notes","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":8,"n":"annotator","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":8,"n":"sentence","v":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/20086235\">pubmed:20086235</a> JNK phosphorylates proteins that are part of AP-1, in particular c-Jun and activating transcription factor 2 (ATF-2). With dominant-negative mutants, antisense RNA, inhibitors, and genetic ablation, it has been shown that JNK and c-Jun play a major role in IL-1–induced expression of genes encoding IL-6 and IL-8 and other IL-1–responsive genes"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":8,"n":"direct","v":"false","d":"boolean"}, {"po":9,"n":"citation","v":["pubmed:21561061"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":9,"n":"mechanism","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":9,"n":"residue","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":9,"n":"sequence","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":9,"n":"cell_data","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":9,"n":"tissue_data","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":9,"n":"notes","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":9,"n":"annotator","v":["Luana"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":9,"n":"sentence","v":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/21561061\">pubmed:21561061</a> AP-1 regulates transcription of many genes involved in viralpathogenesis, including pro-inflammatory and antiviral cytokineslike IL-6,33IL-8,34RANTES,35MCP-1,19interferons,9etc., thatare characteristic of an infection. SARS pathology is the result ofan exacerbated pro-inflammatory immune response by cytokinesin the lungs of patients and in infected animal models."],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":9,"n":"direct","v":"false","d":"boolean"}, {"po":10,"n":"citation","v":["pubmed:22326956"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":10,"n":"mechanism","v":["binding"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":10,"n":"residue","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":10,"n":"sequence","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":10,"n":"cell_data","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":10,"n":"tissue_data","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":10,"n":"notes","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":10,"n":"annotator","v":["miannu"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":10,"n":"sentence","v":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/22326956\">pubmed:22326956</a> TGF-beta signaling mediates a wide range of biological activities in development and disease. TGF-beta ligands signal through heterodimeric type I and type II receptors (TGF-beta receptor type I [TbetaRI, also known as ALK5 and TGFBR1] and TbetaRII) that are members of the serine/threonine kinase family."],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":10,"n":"direct","v":"true","d":"boolean"}, {"po":11,"n":"citation","v":["pubmed:32454942"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":11,"n":"mechanism","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":11,"n":"residue","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":11,"n":"sequence","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":11,"n":"cell_data","v":["BTO:0000801"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":11,"n":"tissue_data","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":11,"n":"notes","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":11,"n":"annotator","v":["miannu"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":11,"n":"sentence","v":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/32454942\">pubmed:32454942</a> Macrophages and microglia show a high plasticity and have been arbitrarily classified into “M1” (proinflammatory) and “M2” (prorepair, anti-inflammatory) phenotypes depending on their activation state, although it is now widely accepted that this classification is hugely oversimplified, particularly for microglia, and only partially reflects the real situation. According to the M1/M2 model, M1 polarized cells are characterized by the release of proinflammatory mediators, such as TNF, IL-1β, and IFNγ"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":11,"n":"direct","v":"false","d":"boolean"}, {"po":12,"n":"citation","v":["pubmed:32454942"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":12,"n":"mechanism","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":12,"n":"residue","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":12,"n":"sequence","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":12,"n":"cell_data","v":["BTO:0000801"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":12,"n":"tissue_data","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":12,"n":"notes","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":12,"n":"annotator","v":["miannu"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":12,"n":"sentence","v":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/32454942\">pubmed:32454942</a> Macrophages and microglia show a high plasticity and have been arbitrarily classified into “M1” (proinflammatory) and “M2” (prorepair, anti-inflammatory) phenotypes depending on their activation state, although it is now widely accepted that this classification is hugely oversimplified, particularly for microglia, and only partially reflects the real situation. According to the M1/M2 model, M1 polarized cells are characterized by the release of proinflammatory mediators, such as TNF, IL-1β, and IFNγ"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":12,"n":"direct","v":"false","d":"boolean"}, {"po":13,"n":"citation","v":["pubmed:32454942"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":13,"n":"mechanism","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":13,"n":"residue","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":13,"n":"sequence","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":13,"n":"cell_data","v":["BTO:0000801"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":13,"n":"tissue_data","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":13,"n":"notes","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":13,"n":"annotator","v":["miannu"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":13,"n":"sentence","v":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/32454942\">pubmed:32454942</a> Macrophages and microglia show a high plasticity and have been arbitrarily classified into “M1” (proinflammatory) and “M2” (prorepair, anti-inflammatory) phenotypes depending on their activation state, although it is now widely accepted that this classification is hugely oversimplified, particularly for microglia, and only partially reflects the real situation. According to the M1/M2 model, M1 polarized cells are characterized by the release of proinflammatory mediators, such as TNF, IL-1β, and IFNγ"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":13,"n":"direct","v":"false","d":"boolean"}, {"po":14,"n":"citation","v":["pubmed:32454942"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":14,"n":"mechanism","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":14,"n":"residue","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":14,"n":"sequence","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":14,"n":"cell_data","v":["BTO:0000801"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":14,"n":"tissue_data","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":14,"n":"notes","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":14,"n":"annotator","v":["miannu"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":14,"n":"sentence","v":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/32454942\">pubmed:32454942</a> Macrophages and microglia show a high plasticity and have been arbitrarily classified into “M1” (proinflammatory) and “M2” (prorepair, anti-inflammatory) phenotypes depending on their activation state, although it is now widely accepted that this classification is hugely oversimplified, particularly for microglia, and only partially reflects the real situation.  M2 polarized cells express a variety of anti-inflammatory mediators, such as IL-4, IL-10, and transforming growth factor-β (TGF-β), and contribute to immunoregulation"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":14,"n":"direct","v":"false","d":"boolean"}, {"po":15,"n":"citation","v":["pubmed:32454942"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":15,"n":"mechanism","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":15,"n":"residue","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":15,"n":"sequence","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":15,"n":"cell_data","v":["BTO:0000801"],"d":"list