o":52,"n":"residue","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":52,"n":"sequence","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":52,"n":"cell_data","v":["BTO:0000801"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":52,"n":"tissue_data","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":52,"n":"notes","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":52,"n":"annotator","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":52,"n":"sentence","v":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/26260587\">pubmed:26260587</a> IL10R2 recruits cytoplasmic protein Jak1 followed by phosphorylation of tyrosine at position 705 in the STAT3 (705Y-STAT3) molecule. Phosphorylated STAT3 forms a homodimer, which is then translocated to the nucleus to facilitate transcriptional regulation of target genes."],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":52,"n":"direct","v":"true","d":"boolean"}, {"po":53,"n":"citation","v":["pubmed:19458083"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":53,"n":"mechanism","v":["phosphorylation"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":53,"n":"residue","v":["Ser423"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":53,"n":"sequence","v":["SPSIRCSsVS"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":53,"n":"cell_data","v":["BTO:0005493","BTO:0000165"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":53,"n":"tissue_data","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":53,"n":"notes","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":53,"n":"annotator","v":["lperfetto"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":53,"n":"sentence","v":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/19458083\">pubmed:19458083</a> A major event leading to smad3 activation is the tgf-beta-induced, tbetari-mediated phosphorylation at two c-terminal serine residues, ser-423 and ser-425, which triggers dissociation of smad3 from its receptors to form a complex with smad4 and accumulate in the nucleus"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":53,"n":"direct","v":"true","d":"boolean"}, {"po":54,"n":"citation","v":["pubmed:9346908"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":54,"n":"mechanism","v":["phosphorylation"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":54,"n":"residue","v":["Ser465"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":54,"n":"sequence","v":["SPSVRCSsMS"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":54,"n":"cell_data","v":["BTO:0001538"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":54,"n":"tissue_data","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":54,"n":"notes","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":54,"n":"annotator","v":["lperfetto"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":54,"n":"sentence","v":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/9346908\">pubmed:9346908</a> Recently, it was demonstrated that Smad2 interacts transiently with and is a direct substrate of the transforming growth factor-_ (TGF-_) type I receptor, T_RI. Phosphorylation sites on smad2 were localized to a carboxyl-terminal fragment containing three serine residues at positions 464, 465, and 467. In this report, we show that T_RI specifically phosphorylates Smad2 on serines 465 and 467.These results indicate that receptor-dependent phosphorylation of Smad2 on serines 465 and 467 is required in mammalian cells to permit association with Smad4 and to propagate TGF-_ signals."],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":54,"n":"direct","v":"true","d":"boolean"}, {"po":55,"n":"citation","v":["pubmed:32454942"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":55,"n":"mechanism","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":55,"n":"residue","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":55,"n":"sequence","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":55,"n":"cell_data","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":55,"n":"tissue_data","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":55,"n":"notes","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":55,"n":"annotator","v":["miannu"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":55,"n":"sentence","v":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/32454942\">pubmed:32454942</a> IL-1β, an inflammatory cytokine primarily expressed in activated macrophages, monocytes, and microglia, significantly contributes to MS development. IL-1β promotes differentiation of T cells into Th17 cells via the STAT3 pathway and thereby promotes and aggravates the inflammatory environment in the CNS"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":55,"n":"direct","v":"false","d":"boolean"}, {"po":56,"n":"citation","v":["pubmed:32024054"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":56,"n":"mechanism","v":["binding"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":56,"n":"residue","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":56,"n":"sequence","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":56,"n":"cell_data","v":["BTO:0001946"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":56,"n":"tissue_data","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":56,"n":"notes","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":56,"n":"annotator","v":["lperfetto"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":56,"n":"sentence","v":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/32024054\">pubmed:32024054</a> Importantly, IL-17 was involved in increased collagen production in cardiac fibroblasts in response to HG, with both subunits of the IL-17RA and IL-17RC heterodimer complex being important to mediating this response."],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":56,"n":"direct","v":"true","d":"boolean"}, {"po":57,"n":"citation","v":["pubmed:18852293"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":57,"n":"mechanism","v":["binding"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":57,"n":"residue","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":57,"n":"sequence","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":57,"n":"cell_data","v":["BTO:0000801"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":57,"n":"tissue_data","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":57,"n":"notes","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":57,"n":"annotator","v":["lperfetto"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":57,"n":"sentence","v":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/18852293\">pubmed:18852293</a> IL-4 signals through the type I (IL-4Ralpha/common gamma-chain [gammac]) and the type II (IL-4Ralpha/-13Ralpha1) IL-4 receptors, whereas IL-13 utilizes only the type II receptor."],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":57,"n":"direct","v":"true","d":"boolean"}, {"po":58,"n":"citation","v":["pubmed:11238858"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":58,"n":"mechanism","v":["binding"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":58,"n":"residue","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":58,"n":"sequence","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":58,"n":"cell_data","v":["BTO:0000785"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":58,"n":"tissue_data","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":58,"n":"notes","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":58,"n":"annotator","v":["gcesareni"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":58,"n":"sentence","v":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/11238858\">pubmed:11238858</a> Part of the receptor for interleukin 6. Binds to il6 with low affinity, but does not transduce a signal. Signal activation necessitate an association with il6st. Activation may lead to the regulation of the immune response, acute-phase reactions and hematopoiesis."],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":58,"n":"direct","v":"true","d":"boolean"}, {"po":59,"n":"citation","v":["pubmed:23663276"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":59,"n":"mechanism","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":59,"n":"residue","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":59,"n":"sequence","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":59,"n":"cell_data","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":59,"n":"tissue_data","v":["BTO:0001103","BTO:0000887"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":59,"n":"notes","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":59,"n":"annotator","v":["milica"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":59,"n":"sentence","v":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/23663276\">pubmed:23663276</a> Il-6 family members typically signal through the common gp130 receptor, with the janus kinase/signal transducer and activator of transcription (jak/stat) pathway being the major intracellular mediator of their effects."],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":59,"n":"direct","v":"true","d":"boolean"}, {"po":60,"n":"citation","v":["pubmed:1340770"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":60,"n":"mechanism","v":["binding"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":60,"n":"residue","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":60,"n":"sequence","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":60,"n":"cell_data","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":60,"n":"tissue_data","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":60,"n":"notes","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":60,"n":"annotator","v":["lperfetto"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":60,"n":"sentence","v":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/1340770\">pubmed:1340770</a> Nf-kappa b is an inducible transcription factor comprised of a 50-kd (p50) and a 65-kd (p65) subunit. Induction of nf-kappa b activity, which is a critical event in many signal transduction pathways, involves release from a cytoplasmic inhibitory protein, i kappa b, followed by translocation of the active transcription factor complex into the nucleus. we demonstrate by in vitro and in vivo methods that the recently cloned i kappa b/mad-3 interacts with both the p50 and p65 subunits of nf-kappa b"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":60,"n":"direct","v":"true","d":"boolean"}, {"po":61,"n":"citation","v":["pubmed:23124025"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":61,"n":"mechanism","v":["phosphorylation"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":61,"n":"residue","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":61,"n":"sequence","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":61,"n":"cell_data","v":["BTO:0000801"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":61,"n":"tissue_data","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":61,"n":"notes","v":[""],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":61,"n":"annotator","v":["lperfetto"],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":61,"n":"sentence","v":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/23124025\">pubmed:23124025</a> Although the receptor-associated tyrosine kinases Jak2 and Tyk2 are activated after the recruitment of IL-13 to its receptor (containing IL-4R and IL-13R1), IL-4 stimulates Jak1 activation."],"d":"list_of_string"}, {"po":61,"n":"direct","v":"true","d":"boolean"}]},
